HL Concept: Living Provenance — Objects That Can Find Their Way Home
MaxSpace Tool Solutions — MSTS Concept documented: May 2026


The Idea
When an object carries a name, it can find its family.

The HL System assigns provenance to objects — not just location and category, but origin. Who owned it. Who used it. What it meant. Where it came from.

Living Provenance is the concept that this provenance, once recorded and made searchable, allows objects to reconnect with their families — even after estate sales, donations, auctions, or decades of separation.


The Problem It Solves
Every day, objects with real family significance pass through estate sales, thrift stores, and auctions — completely anonymized.

A buyer sees a tool, a piece of furniture, a box of hardware. They don't know whose it was. They don't know the name. The family doesn't know it's there.

If the object carried an HL record — even a simple one — that could change.


How It Works
An object in the HL System can carry:

TOOL-WRENCH-COMBINATION-3/4IN

Owner: Dr. Harold Piper

Origin: Sharon, WI — Piper Family Farm

Acquired: ~1940s

Notes: Part of OLG1 cabinet collection

HL-ID: OLG1-FASTENER-WRENCH-001

That record, if made searchable, becomes a bridge.

Scenario A — Estate Sale Discovery: Someone finds a labeled tool at an estate sale. The label carries an HL-ID or a name. They search a public registry. They find the family. They can notify them, sell to them directly, or simply let them see that the object still exists.

Scenario B — Family Research: A family member doing genealogy research on Ancestry.com finds a linked HL record. They can see objects their ancestor owned — not just names and dates, but things. The wrench. The tractor. The cabinet of screws.

Scenario C — Museum and Archive Integration: Historical objects with HL records can be ingested by local historical societies, genealogical databases, or museum archives. The object's story travels with it permanently.


The Ancestry.com Connection
Genealogy databases like Ancestry.com already hold the family trees.

The missing layer is the objects.

A person's family tree currently contains: names, dates, locations, vital records, photographs.

It does not contain: the tools they used, the things they built, the objects they chose to keep.

HL Living Provenance adds that layer.

An HL record linked to an Ancestry profile means:

A family member can see what their great-grandmother owned
An estate sale find can be traced to a specific family line
An object's journey across generations becomes part of the historical record

This integration requires no proprietary technology. It requires only:

A public HL record with a person's name and origin
A searchable registry (could be GitHub, a simple web index, or a dedicated tool)
A matching convention that genealogy platforms can index


What Makes This Different From Existing Solutions
Existing antique and provenance systems focus on monetary value and authentication for high-value items.

Living Provenance focuses on ordinary objects with personal significance — the kind that never make it to Christie's but matter enormously to the family that lost them.

The HL System is designed for the $700 tractor and the drawer of carriage bolts — not the Fabergé egg.


Privacy Model
Objects linked to living people: private by default, shared by choice
Objects linked to deceased people: family-controlled for 10 years, then optionally public
Objects with no living family claim: can be made fully public for historical research


The Vision
Someone walks into an estate sale in Sharon, Wisconsin.

They pick up an old combination wrench. There's a small label on it — faded, but readable. A name. An HL-ID.

They take a photo. They run it through a search. In thirty seconds, they find a family page. They find a great-granddaughter in Milwaukee who didn't know this wrench existed.

She buys it for what it's worth to her — which is more than the estate sale price, and less than the feeling of getting it back.

The object found its way home.

That's Living Provenance.



